#### 2.3.3 Challenges and Solutions
The biggest architectural problem in this task was discovering that any command sent to the server while already logged in was timing out. This turned out to be a pre-existing issue in the client's message handling thread, which only ever processed incoming chat messages and silently dropped anything else, including responses to commands like LIST_USERS or the new VIEW_LOGS. Since account creation and login both happen before the listening thread starts, this bug had never been triggered by any testing up to that point. It was fixed by routing anything that is not a chat message into the same response queue that command sending waits on.

Deciding how the challenge-response mechanism should be used took a few iterations. Gating it on every login as a third factor alongside password and TOTP was one option, but using it instead as the re-authentication step for sensitive actions gave it a clearer, standalone purpose and kept it distinct from TOTP for comparison purposes.

#### 2.3.4 Testing and Validation
The challenge-response mechanism was tested directly: a correct response was accepted, an incorrect one was rejected, and replaying a response after its challenge had already been consumed was also correctly rejected. Role restrictions were tested by attempting LIST_USERS and VIEW_LOGS from both a local user account and a GitHub admin account, confirming the local account was denied both while the admin account succeeded. The failed login alert was tested by entering the wrong password three times in a row and confirming the warning appeared on the server console, then confirming a successful login reset the counter.

